# Users and groups an AURCache build worker needs, declared rather than
# created by a script.
#
# The server package declares `aurcache` in a file of its own. Two sysusers
# files may declare one user -- the second is a no-op -- and they must be two
# files, because pacman refuses to install two packages that ship the same
# path even when the contents are identical.
#
# systemd-sysusers is idempotent by construction and re-applied on boot, where
# an install script has to be written carefully not to clobber an existing
# account -- and would be re-run on every package upgrade.
#
# Two users, deliberately separate:
#
#   aurcache - runs the server and the worker process. Owns the mTLS identity
#              and the build credentials, and is the only user that can read
#              them.
#   builder  - runs each package build. `makechrootpkg -U builder` names it
#              explicitly, so a build never inherits the worker's user through
#              SUDO_USER and cannot reach the worker's secrets by file
#              permissions.
#
# `aurbuild` is builder's PRIMARY group, not a supplementary one, and that is
# load-bearing: makechrootpkg carries only the build user's primary uid/gid into
# the chroot, so a supplementary group does not exist in there and group-write
# on the bind-mounted caches fails with "no write permission for $SRCDEST" --
# from inside the chroot, after every host-side check has already passed.
g aurbuild -
u aurcache - "AURCache server and build worker" /var/lib/aurcache -
# The group goes in the ID field as `-:aurbuild`, which is what makes aurbuild
# builder's PRIMARY group without pinning a uid. A bare `-` would create a
# `builder` group and use that instead -- and that failure shows up only inside
# the chroot, long after every host-side check has passed.
u builder -:aurbuild "AURCache package build user" /var/lib/aurcache-worker -
m aurcache aurbuild
